From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 16:50:11 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: um: provide the AArch64 tagged-address ABI

arm64 Linux lets a task pass pointers whose top byte holds a tag to
system calls once it has asked for that with
prctl(PR_SET_TAGGED_ADDR_CTRL, PR_TAGGED_ADDR_ENABLE)
(Documentation/arch/arm64/tagged-address-abi.rst). UML had neither
prctl, so both failed with EINVAL. Bionic then leaves its heap at
M_HEAP_TAGGING_LEVEL_NONE, no heap pointer carries a tag, and the
Android CTS, which expects every arm64 device without MTE to tag its
heap, fails.

The port now provides the ABI as arm64 Linux 6.18 does on a CPU without
MTE:

- PR_SET_TAGGED_ADDR_CTRL and PR_GET_TAGGED_ADDR_CTRL, the
  TIF_TAGGED_ADDR flag they keep (inherited by fork and clone, cleared
  by exec), EINVAL for an AArch32 task, the abi.tagged_addr_disabled
  sysctl, and NT_ARM_TAGGED_ADDR_CTRL for a tracer.
- untagged_addr(): the top byte goes unless bit 55 is set.
  untagged_addr_remote() is the generic one. The generic callers that
  untag now see it: mprotect, madvise, mincore, mlock, munlock, msync,
  munmap, the old address of mremap, GUP (process_vm_readv and
  process_vm_writev, ptrace PEEK and POKE) and pagemap. Native untags
  these whether or not the task enabled the ABI.
- access_ok() untags for a task that enabled the ABI and for a kernel
  thread, which has no flag of its own. The skas uaccess copy and the
  futex operations untag the address they map, as the LDTR and STTR
  accesses of the native kernel ignore the tag (TBI0).
- A fault's si_addr is untagged, except for a TRAP_BRKPT watchpoint
  (arch_untagged_si_addr()).
- process_vm_writev() takes the remote address as the caller gave it.
  Once GUP untags it, the pages pin, and the code invalidation that
  follows must name the same address: the host rejected the tagged one
  and the kernel panicked ("unable to invalidate guest mm code").
  darwin_flush_icache_remote_user_range() untags.

The host's engines ignore the top byte of a data address when it runs
with top-byte-ignore. A fault they report is the address they
translated, so the tag is already gone, and segv() must not untag it: a
host without top-byte-ignore faults at the tagged address, and mapping
the untagged page would only make the same access fault again. The
comment in segv() says so. As a result si_addr never carries a tag,
even with SA_EXPOSE_TAGBITS.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
